<div class="evidence-note"><p><strong>Benchmark correction, 11 September 2026.</strong> Earlier versions of this page presented age-specific seconds and percentiles as established norms. We could not substantiate those claims from the cited sources. They have been removed. This page now separates published grip-strength evidence from personal training goals.</p></div>
<div class="callout" id="short-answer"><p><strong>Short answer</strong></p><p>No large study has measured dead hang time across the population, so there is no verified average. The main measured sample is 25 recreational athletes aged about 20 (GRIP-6, 2025): men held 64.0 seconds and women 48.4 seconds on average, with wide variation. A second, older sample of 32 moderately trained men is below. Compare your own hold using the <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">dead hang calculator</a>. For the force side, see the <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength guide</a>.</p></div>
