</div>
<p class="bya-updated">Updated 30 October 2026. The GRIP-6 sample is young and trained. Attia's numbers are a podcast target for very strong people. Neither is a population average.</p>
<h2 id="average-times">Dead hang times by age: what the chart can tell you</h2>
<p>An age chart is only as useful as the test behind it. The same person can record different hang times on different bars or with different assistance. Keep the testing conditions consistent before interpreting a change.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Age and hang-time interpretation"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Age group</th><th scope="col">What to use</th><th scope="col">What not to infer</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['Adults under 40','Your own baseline with a consistent grip and bar.','An exact percentile from a generic internet seconds chart.'],['Adults 40 to 59','Comparable holds and a record of assistance used.','A fixed decline in hanging seconds each decade.'],['Adults 60 and over','A controlled, suitable variation and individual advice where needed.','A required unassisted hold or a lifespan prediction.']].map(([age,use,avoid])=><tr><th scope="row">{age}</th><td>{use}</td><td>{avoid}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h3 id="measured-sample">Measured hang times in a published study</h3>
<p>In a 2025 study of 25 trained recreational athletes aged about 20, men held a passive bar hang for 64 seconds on average and women for 48 seconds, with wide individual variation. No large population study of dead hang time exists, so treat these as one measured sample, not a norm.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Dead hang times measured in the GRIP-6 study"><table><caption>Passive bar hang time, mean (standard deviation), seconds. Chomiuk et al., GRIP-6 study, 2025, Table 2.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">People</th><th scope="col">Before 6 weeks of training</th><th scope="col">After 6 weeks of training</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Men</th><td>11</td><td>64.0 (35.3)</td><td>69.4 (33.7)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Women</th><td>14</td><td>48.4 (29.1)</td><td>54.3 (28.7)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Whole group</th><td>25</td><td>55.3</td><td>60.9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="visual-block bya-chart"><div class="dg-chart dg-range" role="img" aria-label="Dead hang times in the GRIP-6 study. Mean hang time with one standard deviation either side. Men before training 64.0 seconds, after 69.4. Women before training 48.4 seconds, after 54.3. The bars overlap heavily, so individual times vary widely."><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Men, before</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:53.33%">64.0 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:23.92%;width:58.83%"></i><i class="dg-dot open" style="left:53.33%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Men, after</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:57.83%">69.4 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:29.75%;width:56.17%"></i><i class="dg-dot solid" style="left:57.83%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Women, before</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:40.33%">48.4 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:16.08%;width:48.5%"></i><i class="dg-dot open" style="left:40.33%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow"><span class="dg-rlabel">Women, after</span><div class="dg-rtrack"><span class="dg-val" style="left:45.25%">54.3 s</span><i class="dg-whisk" style="left:21.33%;width:47.83%"></i><i class="dg-dot solid" style="left:45.25%"></i></div></div><div class="dg-rrow dg-raxisrow"><span class="dg-rlabel" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="dg-raxis"><span style="left:0%">0 s</span><span style="left:25%">30 s</span><span style="left:50%">60 s</span><span style="left:75%">90 s</span><span style="left:100%">120 s</span></div></div></div><p class="caption">Dots are means (open before training, filled after). Bars span one standard deviation either side of the mean. GRIP-6 study, 2025.</p></div>
<p>The standard deviation is more than half the mean for both sexes, so individual times in this sample ranged widely. An older and smaller test gives a second reference point: 32 moderately trained men averaged 95.4 seconds on a standard bar and 107.6 seconds on a different bar design, the Tri-Bar (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1519/R-13883.1" rel="noopener">Drury et al., 2004</a>). That is a male-only sample on two bars, so it is not a norm either.</p>
<div class="evidence-note bya-method"><p><strong>How the GRIP-6 hangs were measured.</strong> Overhand grip at shoulder width, legs off the ground, straight arms, hanging passively until the person dropped. Three trials were averaged, with three minutes of rest between them. The 25 participants were healthy recreational athletes with a mean age of 20.4, 11 men and 14 women. Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/jfmk10040427" rel="noopener">Chomiuk et al., J Funct Morphol Kinesiol 2025;10(4):427</a>. A young trained sample is not a guide to every age, so compare older or untrained hangs with care.</p></div>
<h2 id="male-standards">Men’s times and women’s times</h2>
<p id="female-standards">Published handgrip-force norms differ by age and sex. Those measurements use a dynamometer and report force. Supporting bodyweight from a bar for time involves another task. Applying the force percentiles directly to hang duration would be misleading.</p>
<p><a href="https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0113637">Dodds et al. explain the instruments and populations in their study</a>. That paper does not provide the age-specific dead-hang seconds previously displayed here.</p>
<h2 id="expert-targets">Expert hang-time targets for men and women</h2>
<p>Peter Attia said on his podcast in December 2024 that a "very strong" 40-year-old man should be able to dead hang for at least two minutes and a "very strong" 40-year-old woman for at least 90 seconds. That is his target for strong people, not an average. In the GRIP-6 study, 11 men averaged 64 seconds and 14 women 48 seconds, at a mean age of about 20.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Expert targets compared with measured averages"><table><caption>Targets and measured means side by side. They describe different people.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Figure</th><th scope="col">Who</th><th scope="col">What it represents</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">At least 120 s</th><td>"Very strong" 40-year-old man</td><td>Expert target</td><td><a href="https://peterattiamd.com/specialama/" rel="noopener">Attia, The Drive #329</a></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">At least 90 s</th><td>"Very strong" 40-year-old woman</td><td>Expert target</td><td><a href="https://peterattiamd.com/specialama/" rel="noopener">Attia, The Drive #329</a></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">64.0 s</th><td>11 trained men, mean age about 20</td><td>Measured mean</td><td><a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/jfmk10040427" rel="noopener">GRIP-6, 2025</a></td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">48.4 s</th><td>14 trained women, mean age about 20</td><td>Measured mean</td><td><a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/jfmk10040427" rel="noopener">GRIP-6, 2025</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>He also said to subtract about 10 seconds per decade. That is a rule of thumb from a podcast, not measured data, so this page does not turn it into an age table. The wording comes from the <a href="https://podscripts.co/podcasts/the-peter-attia-drive/329-special-ama-peter-on-exercise-important-labs-building-good-habits-promising-longevity-research-and-more" rel="noopener">episode transcript</a>; the show notes are at <a href="https://peterattiamd.com/specialama/" rel="noopener">peterattiamd.com</a>.</p>
<h2 id="percentile-chart">Why we do not give dead-hang percentiles</h2>
<p>A percentile needs an appropriate comparison population and a standardized test. We do not have that dataset for these timed hangs. A result such as 60 seconds cannot truthfully become “top 25%” simply by assigning a band to it.</p>
<p>The calculator instead shows what fraction of your own target you have reached. A 30-second hold against a 60-second target is 50% of that target. It says nothing about how many people you outperform.</p>
<h2 id="is-my-time-good">Is your time useful to build from?</h2>
<p>A controlled hold gives you a starting record. A feet-supported hold is also recordable; keep it separate from an unassisted hold. Short holds are not failures. End the attempt while you can step down safely.</p>
<p>10, 30, 60 and 120 seconds are convenient round-number milestones. They are not validated age cut-offs or required fitness standards. Choose your next target and use the <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal hang calculator</a> to compare it.</p>
<h3 id="what-does-your-time-mean">What does your time mean?</h3>
<p>A one-minute dead hang is a little under the 64-second mean of the men in the GRIP-6 study (11 men, mean age about 20) and above the 48-second mean of its women. It is well under Attia's two-minute target for a very strong 40-year-old man. Those are different groups, so this is a rough comparison, not a ranking.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where common hang times sit against the sourced reference points"><table><caption>Where a hang time sits against the sourced reference points only. No percentiles.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Your time</th><th scope="col">Against the measured means (men 64.0 s, women 48.4 s)</th><th scope="col">Against Attia's targets (man 120 s, woman 90 s)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">10 to 20 s</th><td>Below both means</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">30 s</th><td>Below both means</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">45 s</th><td>Just under the women's mean, below the men's</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">60 s</th><td>Above the women's mean, just under the men's</td><td>Below both</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">90 s</th><td>Above both means</td><td>Meets the woman's target, below the man's</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">120 s</th><td>Above both means</td><td>Meets both targets</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">3 minutes or more</th><td>Above both means</td><td>Above both targets, far below the 80:41 record</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Individual times in the study varied by roughly 30 to 35 seconds (one standard deviation) either side of the means, so a gap of 10 seconds says little. If you are starting out, the <a href="/beginner-deadhang/">beginner dead hang guide</a> covers short holds, and the <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">calculator</a> tracks progress against a target you choose.</p>
<div class="visual-block bya-chart"><div class="dg-chart dg-ladder-chart" role="img" aria-label="Reference ladder from 0 to 180 seconds. Measured means: women 48.4 seconds, men 64.0 seconds. Attia targets for a very strong 40-year-old: women at least 90 seconds, men at least 120 seconds. The Guinness record of 80 minutes 41 seconds is off the scale."><div class="dg-lplot"><p class="dg-lane">Measured means, trained athletes aged about 20</p><div class="dg-ltrack"><i class="dg-dot solid" style="left:26.89%"></i><b class="dg-pt up" style="left:26.89%">Women 48.4 s</b><i class="dg-dot solid" style="left:35.56%"></i><b class="dg-pt down" style="left:35.56%">Men 64.0 s</b></div><p class="dg-lane">Attia targets, very strong 40-year-olds (at least)</p><div class="dg-ltrack"><i class="dg-dot diamond" style="left:50%"></i><b class="dg-pt up" style="left:50%">Women 90 s+</b><i class="dg-dot diamond" style="left:66.67%"></i><b class="dg-pt down" style="left:66.67%">Men 120 s+</b></div><div class="dg-laxis"><span style="left:0%">0 s</span><span style="left:16.67%">30 s</span><span style="left:33.33%">60 s <small>(1 min)</small></span><span style="left:50%">90 s</span><span style="left:66.67%">120 s</span><span style="left:83.33%">150 s</span><span style="left:100%">180 s</span></div></div><p class="dg-guinness">Guinness record: 80 min 41 s (4,841 s), far off the scale &#8594;</p></div><p class="caption">Circles are measured means (GRIP-6, 2025). Diamonds are expert targets (Attia, 2024). Dashed line marks one minute.</p></div>
<h2 id="age-decline">What changes when you repeat the test?</h2>
<p>Record your bar, grip and assistance consistently. A different bar diameter, chalk or foot support changes the conditions. Compare like with like. A lower result on one day is not a diagnosis of declining health.</p>
<h3 id="does-hang-time-fall-with-age">Does hang time fall with age?</h3>
<p>Grip force falls with age, but nobody has measured how many hanging seconds are lost per decade. A pooled analysis of 12 British studies (60,803 measurements from 49,964 participants) found median peak grip of 51 kg in males aged 29 to 39 and 31 kg in females aged 26 to 42 (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0113637" rel="noopener">Dodds et al., PLOS ONE 2014</a>). That is force in kilograms, not seconds. The <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength guide</a> covers the force side.</p>
<h2 id="improve">Turn your baseline into a routine</h2>
<p>Choose manageable holds and enough rest to maintain control. Record what you actually held instead of saving the timer setting as a result. The <a href="/training-programs/">training plans</a> organize practice and review points without promising a particular gain.</p>
<h2 id="how-long-per-session">How long should you dead hang in a session?</h2>
<p>Most people do 2 to 4 hangs per session, each held at a controlled effort, with 60 to 90 seconds of rest between hangs, and train on three or more days a week. This is common training practice on this site, not the result of a trial. Beginners often start with 10 to 20 second holds.</p>
<p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="General session guidance by level"><table><caption>General practice, not research. Based on this site's frequency and program guides.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Days per week</th><th scope="col">Hangs per session</th><th scope="col">Each hang</th><th scope="col">Rest between</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Beginner</th><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>10 to 20 s</td><td>60 to 90 s</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Intermediate</th><td>4 to 5</td><td>3 to 4</td><td>20 to 45 s</td><td>60 to 90 s</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Advanced</th><td>5 to 7</td><td>3 to 4</td><td>30 to 60 s</td><td>60 to 90 s</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Stop a hang before your grip gives out unexpectedly, and stop if you feel sharp pain. See <a href="/deadhang-frequency/">how often to dead hang</a> for the reasoning and <a href="/training-programs/">the training programs</a> for week-by-week plans. This is not medical advice.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Common questions about hang time</h2>{allFaqs.map(([q,a])=><details class="answer"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}
<h2 id="sources">Sources and method</h2><p>The hang times come from the GRIP-6 study (Chomiuk et al., 2025). Dodds et al. (2014) supplies the grip-force figures, which measure squeeze force in kilograms, not hanging time. The personal-goal examples on this page are arithmetic and editorial milestones, not research-derived training prescriptions.</p><p>Read our <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a> for how we distinguish research, interpretation and equipment advice.</p>
